    </patterns>
  </knowledge>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0" name="Context + rigor declaration">Load the break-assessment + CVE memo + spec FR-03/NFR-03 + design §6.4. Declare RIGOR: FULL. Run `/conflict-check` (BFF hot path).</step>
    <step order="1">Bump `Microsoft.Graph` 5.101.0 → 6.5.0 (re-pull latest 6.x first); restore; inspect the resolved graph — confirm `Graph.Core 4.0.1` + transitive `Microsoft.Kiota.* 2.0.x`.</step>
    <step order="2">Delete all 7 direct `Microsoft.Kiota.*` `PackageReference`s; restore again; `dotnet list package --include-transitive | grep -i kiota` — confirm a single 2.0.x across all Kiota assemblies. Confirm `NoWarn=NU1903` is absent (add nothing back).</step>
    <step order="3">`dotnet build -c Release src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/`. Resolve the `ServiceException` swing factor: if retained in Graph.Core 4.x, build passes as-is; if removed, delete the ~72 dead `ServiceException` catch blocks (6 files) per the assessment.</step>
    <step order="4">Fix the latent `DriveItemOperations` bug: re-anchor its dead `ServiceException` 404/403 catches on `ODataError` so those statuses surface correctly (the assessment flagged this as a natural in-passing fix).</step>
    <step order="5">Run the Graph-affected tests (unit + the Dataverse/SPE/auth seam integration tests); re-smoke the four auth paths (OBO, Managed Identity, named API-key) + SPE file ops + mail/communication + a background job tick. Record the two behavior-level verify items (`ODataError.ResponseHeaders`; CAE/backing-store) as observations.</step>
    <step order="6">Update `src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/CLAUDE.md` Package Management section: Kiota is now transitive at 2.0.x via Graph 6.5 (no direct pins); the version-match invariant is satisfied transitively.</step>
    <step order="7">Update `tasks/TASK-INDEX.md`: set 033 status to ✅. Note the publish-size delta for task 031.</step>
    <step order="8">Document any deviation in `projects/dotnet-10-upgrade-r1/notes/`.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If ANY call site turns out to be a deep/semantic change (not the mechanical rename/version bump the assessment predicted) — e.g. `ServiceException` is removed AND its catches are load-bearing (not dead), or a direct-Kiota usage hits a 2.0 break the grep missed, or an auth/serialization behavior actually changes — STOP and surface per CLAUDE.md §6/§6.5. The fold-in was approved on the "mechanical break" premise; a deep break is a scope change the owner must re-decide (defer back out is a valid outcome).</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <tools>
    <tool name="dotnet">Build + restore + vulnerable/transitive audit</tool>
  </tools>

